/// The tomakast plugin contract, version 0.1.
///
/// A plugin is a WebAssembly component that implements the `plugin` world:
/// the launcher calls its five exports and the plugin answers with *effects*,
/// things it asks the launcher to do (show a view, a toast, copy text,
/// replace its indexed items, redraw its widgets). Effects are the only way to
/// change the launcher; the imports give access to the plugin's own storage, its
/// preferences, opening links and files, web requests, timers, logging, the
/// user's time zone and the computer's approximate location.
///
/// Permissions. A plugin gets nothing beyond the permissions in its
/// `plugin.toml` that the user approved. The host checks them on every import
/// call and on every effect: `copy-text` needs `clipboard`, `replace-items`
/// needs `index`, and `search` is called only with `search`. `preferences`,
/// `log` and `locale` need no permission; `storage`, `open`, `http`,
/// `timers` and `location` need their own. Showing widgets needs no permission. Some things
/// also need a user action, that is a call the user started: `run`, `on-event`
/// with an `action-chosen` or `form-submitted` view event, or `on-event` with
/// a `widget` event that is a user action: `action`, `key` or a completed
/// click (see `widget-event`). `query-changed`, `text-changed` and `closed` are
/// not user actions (`closed` is also sent when the launcher hides), nor are a
/// widget's `shown`, `hidden`, a pointer `down` alone or an `up` that completes
/// no click, `move`, `enter` and `leave`, nor is `home`. `copy-text` and the
/// `open` functions need a user action, and one call may open at most once. A
/// denied effect is dropped. The host shows at most one denial toast per plugin
/// per 10 s; it is the host's own toast and does not count towards the plugin's
/// toast limit (see `toast`). WASI gives no filesystem, environment, arguments,
/// stdio or sockets; it gives the wall clock, the monotonic clock and random
/// numbers, and nothing else.
///
/// Deadlines and limits. `search` must answer within 100 ms, `init` within
/// 2000 ms, `run` and `on-event` within 1000 ms. Linear memory is capped at
/// 64 MiB. A trap, an exceeded deadline or an exceeded limit marks the plugin
/// broken: the user sees an error toast and the plugin is unloaded. A broken
/// plugin stays unloaded until its folder changes or the launcher restarts.
/// `render-widget` should answer within 16 ms: a later frame is dropped, and
/// after 5 late or failed frames in a row the widget is stopped. Past 100 ms
/// the plugin is broken, as for `search`. Render calls count against no
/// background budget.
///
/// Background budget. What the host starts on the plugin's behalf, `on-event`
/// for a timer, an HTTP answer or a location, may use at most 1 s per 10 s of time spent in
/// the call (including host imports). Over that, the plugin's timers are
/// suspended for 60 s (nothing of them fires; their waiting events are dropped
/// and one-shot timers fire after the suspension); the third time within
/// 10 minutes the plugin is stopped as broken.
///
/// Lifecycle. The host may unload an idle plugin and load it again later, so
/// a plugin keeps no state that it cannot restore from `storage`. (Unloading
/// idle plugins is not implemented in 0.1, but plugins must not rely on that.)
///
/// Size caps and validity. Before the launcher sees an effect, the host
/// checks the caps stated on the records below and the rules for
/// `action.shortcut`, `icon.emoji` and `icon.color`. An effect that breaks
/// any of them is rejected as a whole, with an error toast. The value one
/// call returns is at most 4 MiB in total; a larger value cannot be taken
/// out of the plugin at all, so it marks the plugin broken, like a trap.
/// The same bound applies to the arguments of one import call. Every id
/// (item, action, view,
/// row, tile, field) is at most 256 bytes and contains no control
/// characters and no Unicode format characters (category Cf: bidi
/// overrides, zero-width characters, tags); titles and other display text
/// may contain format characters. Every title, subtitle, label, caption,
/// placeholder, section, error and accessory is at most 1 KiB and one line:
/// no control characters (no newlines or tabs). Markdown, metadata values,
/// field values, scratchpad text and toast messages may span lines.
///
/// Shape. WIT rejects recursive types, so views are flat: no view contains
/// another view, and sections are a field on rows. Tuples are expressed as
/// records (`key-value`, `dropdown-option`).
package tomakast:plugin@0.1.0;

interface types {
  /// An action the user can run on an item, row, tile or view. The first
  /// action of a list is the primary one (Enter).
  ///
  /// `shortcut` is an optional key combination that runs the action while
  /// its row is selected, written as modifiers and one key joined by `+`:
  /// `ctrl+shift+c`, `alt+f2`, `super+n`. Modifiers are `ctrl` (`control`),
  /// `alt`, `shift` and `super` (`meta`, `logo`, `win`), in any case. So that
  /// a plugin cannot take the keys the user types and navigates with, a
  /// shortcut:
  /// - includes `ctrl`, `alt` or `super` (`shift` alone is not enough);
  /// - does not use Tab, Backspace, Delete, Home, End, the arrows, Enter,
  ///   Esc or Space, with any modifiers;
  /// - is not Ctrl alone with A, C, V, X, Z, Y or K (`ctrl+shift+c` is
  ///   allowed).
  ///
  /// A shortcut that does not parse or breaks these rules rejects the whole
  /// effect, with an error toast. A valid shortcut that still collides with
  /// a launcher key is ignored: the action runs only from the action panel.
  ///
  /// A row, tile, item or view has at most 16 actions.
  record action {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    shortcut: option<string>,
  }

  /// An icon or a tile image.
  /// - `named`: the name of an icon in the launcher's bundled icon set,
  ///   Lucide (lucide.dev), such as `bell` or `calculator`; it follows the
  ///   id rule. A Lucide name draws the same icon on every OS. A name the set
  ///   does not have is looked up in the desktop's icon theme on Linux (the
  ///   freedesktop icon theme, then `hicolor`); a name found in neither, and
  ///   any such name on macOS and Windows, shows the item's monogram, as an
  ///   item without an icon does.
  /// - `plugin-file`: an image file inside the plugin's own folder, as a
  ///   relative path that follows the id rule. The host rejects absolute
  ///   paths and any `..` component. It resolves the path against the plugin
  ///   folder; when the file is missing, is not a regular file, is larger
  ///   than 1 MiB, or the path goes through a symbolic link, the icon is
  ///   dropped (shown as no icon) and the effect is kept. Only PNG and JPEG
  ///   images of at most 1024x1024 pixels (as declared in their header) are
  ///   drawn; any other file shows as no icon.
  /// - `emoji`: an emoji or symbol, drawn as text at icon size: at most
  ///   32 bytes, no control characters, and no format characters except the
  ///   zero-width joiner U+200D (ZWJ sequences such as 👨‍💻) and the tag
  ///   characters U+E0020–U+E007F (subdivision flags such as England's). It
  ///   has at least one character that is neither of these: a joiner or tags
  ///   alone are refused.
  /// - `color`: a colour swatch, written `#rrggbb` (six hex digits).
  ///
  /// An `emoji` or `color` that breaks these rules rejects the whole effect,
  /// with an error toast.
  variant icon {
    named(string),
    plugin-file(string),
    emoji(string),
    color(string),
  }

  /// An indexed or dynamic item a plugin hands to the launcher.
  ///
  /// `id` must be `<plugin-id>/<local-id>`: items whose id has another
  /// prefix are dropped. The host sets the item's kind to "command" and its
  /// source, the label shown on the row, to the plugin's name from
  /// `plugin.toml`; a plugin cannot set either.
  ///
  /// `actions` must not be empty; the first one is the primary action. At
  /// most 32 keywords of at most 1 KiB each, and 16 actions.
  record item {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    subtitle: option<string>,
    keywords: list<string>,
    icon: option<icon>,
    actions: list<action>,
  }

  record key-value {
    key: string,
    value: string,
  }

  /// `value` follows the id rule.
  record dropdown-option {
    value: string,
    label: string,
  }

  /// Who filters a list as the user types: the launcher (`core-filter`,
  /// over row titles) or the plugin (`module`, through `query-changed`
  /// events and `update-view`).
  enum search-mode {
    core-filter,
    module,
  }

  variant accessory {
    text(string),
    tag(string),
  }

  /// One row of a `list-view`.
  ///
  /// Rows of one section are contiguous: the launcher draws a section header
  /// above each row whose `section` is set and differs from the section
  /// above it, and a row with `section: none` continues the previous
  /// section.
  ///
  /// `detail-markdown` is at most 64 KiB; at most 8 accessories and 16
  /// actions.
  record list-row {
    id: string,
    section: option<string>,
    title: string,
    subtitle: option<string>,
    icon: option<icon>,
    accessories: list<accessory>,
    detail-markdown: option<string>,
    actions: list<action>,
  }

  /// A searchable list, with an optional detail pane for the selected row.
  ///
  /// At most 2000 rows; `query` is at most 64 KiB.
  ///
  /// Revision rule: `revision` counts the user's edits of the search field.
  /// The launcher starts the count at the view's `revision` and adds one per
  /// edit; each `query-changed` event carries the new value. An
  /// `update-view` of a list is applied only when its `revision` equals the
  /// last revision the launcher sent, and it never changes what the user
  /// typed: its `query` is ignored.
  record list-view {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    placeholder: string,
    search: search-mode,
    query: string,
    revision: u64,
    rows: list<list-row>,
    show-detail: bool,
  }

  /// One tile of a `grid-view`; at most 16 actions.
  record grid-tile {
    id: string,
    image: option<icon>,
    caption: string,
    actions: list<action>,
  }

  /// A grid of tiles. `columns` is at most 12; `0` means 1. At most 500 tiles.
  record grid-view {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    columns: u8,
    tiles: list<grid-tile>,
  }

  /// Markdown with metadata and actions. `markdown` is at most 64 KiB; at
  /// most 64 metadata entries, each value at most 4 KiB, and 16 actions.
  ///
  /// Markdown in any view is inert: raw HTML is shown as text, remote images
  /// are not loaded, and only `http`, `https` and `mailto` links open, on
  /// click, and only if the plugin holds the `open` permission.
  record detail-view {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    markdown: string,
    metadata: list<key-value>,
    actions: list<action>,
  }

  /// `value` is at most 64 KiB.
  record text-field {
    value: string,
    placeholder: string,
  }

  /// At most 200 options; `value` follows the id rule.
  record dropdown-field {
    options: list<dropdown-option>,
    value: string,
  }

  /// A `date` is empty (`none` or `""`) or has the shape `YYYY-MM-DD`
  /// (four digits, a month 01–12, a day 01–31; the calendar is not checked).
  /// A `multiline` value is at most 256 KiB.
  variant field-kind {
    text(text-field),
    multiline(string),
    checkbox(bool),
    dropdown(dropdown-field),
    date(option<string>),
  }

  /// One form field. `error`, when set, is shown under the field; a plugin
  /// sets or clears it with an `update-view` of the form (for example after a
  /// `form-submitted` it rejects).
  record form-field {
    id: string,
    label: string,
    kind: field-kind,
    error: option<string>,
  }

  /// A form. At most 50 fields.
  ///
  /// An `update-view` of a form is dropped as a whole unless it has the same
  /// fields, with the same ids and kinds, in the same order. It may change
  /// labels, dropdown options, errors and the submit title. It replaces only
  /// the values of fields the user has not touched: what the user entered is
  /// kept.
  record form-view {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    fields: list<form-field>,
    submit-title: string,
  }

  /// A note shown at the end of line `line` of a scratchpad. `line` is
  /// 0-based and below 10 000; when several annotations name the same line,
  /// the last one wins. `text` is at most 1 KiB.
  record line-annotation {
    line: u32,
    text: string,
    is-error: bool,
    /// A colour square drawn before `text`: `#rrggbb` or `#rrggbbaa` (hex digits, any
    /// case). Breaking this rule rejects the whole effect with an error toast, as a bad
    /// `icon.color` does.
    swatch: option<string>,
  }

  /// A free text editor with per-line annotations (a calculator pad).
  /// `text` is at most 256 KiB; at most 10 000 annotations and 16 actions.
  ///
  /// `actions` act on the pad as a whole, as in a detail view: the `row-id`
  /// of their `action-chosen` is the view's id. Enter types a newline in the
  /// editor, so the actions run from the action panel, by their shortcuts,
  /// and the first one by Ctrl+Enter (Cmd+Enter on macOS). An edit still
  /// waiting to be sent arrives as `text-changed` before the action's
  /// `action-chosen`. An `update-view` replaces them.
  ///
  /// Revision rule: `revision` counts the user's edits, as for `list-view`;
  /// each `text-changed` event carries the new value. An `update-view` of a
  /// scratchpad is applied only when its `revision` equals the last revision
  /// the launcher sent, and its `text` is ignored: it updates the
  /// annotations, the output, the toolbar and the title, never what the
  /// user typed.
  ///
  /// `output`, when set, makes the pad a compose pad (a translator): two
  /// columns, each the whole height and scrolling on its own, the editor
  /// (wrapping long lines, no annotation column: `annotations` are ignored)
  /// and beside it a pane showing `output` as markdown. `output` is at most
  /// 256 KiB. With `none` the pad is a sheet as above.
  ///
  /// `toolbar` is a compose pad's buttons, in its header in place of the
  /// title: the first half over the editor, the second half over the pane,
  /// a middle one (of an odd count) between them. At most 8 (see
  /// `toolbar-button`); a sheet shows none.
  ///
  /// `busy` says the pad is working on its text (a translation on its way):
  /// the launcher shows that under the header, with a moving line, or a still
  /// one when the system asks for reduced motion. Put no "loading" words in
  /// the title for it.
  ///
  /// `placeholder` is shown, muted, in the empty editor (empty: none), at
  /// most 1 KiB and one line; it is set when the pad is pushed.
  ///
  /// A compose pad's text and its `output` share one text size, in a few
  /// steps from the body size up to about twice it: the largest at which the
  /// text fits its column (the placeholder on one line), so a short text
  /// reads large. The output does not count: an answer does not resize it. `detail` (markdown, at most 256 KiB with
  /// `output`) follows `output` in its column at the body size: notes on the
  /// output, such as a dictionary, that should not grow with it.
  record scratchpad-view {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    text: string,
    revision: u64,
    annotations: list<line-annotation>,
    actions: list<action>,
    output: option<string>,
    toolbar: list<toolbar-button>,
    busy: bool,
    placeholder: string,
    detail: option<string>,
  }

  /// One choice in a toolbar button's menu. `id` follows the id rule;
  /// `title` and `subtitle` are one line of at most 1 KiB; at most 8
  /// `keywords`, each a title. The menu's filter matches the title, the
  /// subtitle and the keywords alike (a language's names in other
  /// languages).
  record menu-option {
    id: string,
    title: string,
    subtitle: option<string>,
    icon: option<icon>,
    keywords: list<string>,
  }

  /// A button in a compose pad's toolbar. `id` follows the id rule; `label`
  /// is one line of at most 1 KiB; `shortcut` follows the rules of
  /// `action.shortcut` and runs the button as a click does.
  ///
  /// - Without `options` it is a plain button: a click sends `action-chosen`
  ///   with the view's id as `row-id` and the button's id as `action-id`,
  ///   as a pad action does.
  /// - With `options` (at most 300) a click opens its menu, a pop-up under
  ///   the button with a filter field; `selected` names the option shown as
  ///   current. Choosing one sends `action-chosen` with the option's id as
  ///   `row-id` and the button's id as `action-id`. Opening the menu sends
  ///   nothing.
  ///
  /// Either way, an edit still waiting to be sent arrives as `text-changed`
  /// first. A cap broken, or a bad shortcut, rejects the whole effect.
  ///
  /// A pad action (in Ctrl/Cmd+K) whose `id` is a button's runs that button
  /// instead, from the panel and by its shortcut: a button with options opens
  /// its menu. So the panel can list the bar's buttons with their keys.
  record toolbar-button {
    id: string,
    label: string,
    icon: option<icon>,
    shortcut: option<string>,
    options: list<menu-option>,
    selected: option<string>,
  }

  /// A plugin-owned view. Its `id` names it in events and updates. The stack
  /// holds at most one view of a plugin with a given id: pushing an id the
  /// plugin already has on the stack returns to that view (see `push-view`).
  /// The launcher's footer shows the plugin's name, so a view cannot pass
  /// itself off as the launcher's; the header shows the view's title.
  variant view {
    list-view(list-view),
    grid-view(grid-view),
    detail-view(detail-view),
    form-view(form-view),
    scratchpad-view(scratchpad-view),
  }

  variant field-value {
    text(string),
    boolean(bool),
    date(option<string>),
  }

  record form-entry {
    field-id: string,
    value: field-value,
  }

  /// Another reading of the query than as typed.
  enum query-reading-kind {
    /// The same keys on another layout: "ашкуащч" → "firefox", "afqks" → "файлы".
    layout,
    /// A romanization of a non-Latin query: "опен" → "open".
    translit,
  }

  record query-reading {
    kind: query-reading-kind,
    text: string,
  }

  /// `readings`: other readings of `text`, at most 8, never equal to `text`
  /// or to each other.
  record query-changed {
    revision: u64,
    text: string,
    readings: list<query-reading>,
  }

  /// The user ran `action-id`. `row-id` is the id of the chosen row in a
  /// list, of the chosen tile in a grid, and the view's own id in a detail
  /// view or a scratchpad.
  record action-chosen {
    row-id: string,
    action-id: string,
  }

  record text-changed {
    revision: u64,
    text: string,
  }

  /// An event from one of the plugin's views.
  ///
  /// `closed` is sent once when the view leaves the screen: it was popped, or
  /// the launcher window was hidden (then every view on the stack is closed).
  /// A scratchpad's last edit arrives as `text-changed` before its `closed`.
  /// Stop the timers that refresh the view and save what must persist; the
  /// view will not be shown again.
  variant view-event {
    query-changed(query-changed),
    action-chosen(action-chosen),
    form-submitted(list<form-entry>),
    text-changed(text-changed),
    closed,
  }

  enum toast-kind {
    success,
    error,
    progress,
  }

  /// A short notice at the bottom of the launcher. A `success` toast stays
  /// 3 s, an `error` toast 5 s, and a `progress` toast until another toast
  /// replaces it. A toast is shown only while the launcher window is open.
  /// A plugin may show at most 10 toasts per 10 s; the rest are dropped.
  /// (The host's own denial and error toasts about a plugin do not count.)
  /// `message` is at most 4 KiB. The title is shown as written. When a plugin
  /// copies text in answer to the user, the launcher says so itself ("Copied
  /// to clipboard"): a plugin needs no toast of its own for that.
  record toast {
    kind: toast-kind,
    title: string,
    message: option<string>,
  }

  /// How wide a tile is: `quarter`, a quarter of the strip's width; `single`, half of it;
  /// `double`, its whole width. Every tile is as high as the strip.
  enum widget-size {
    quarter,
    single,
    double,
  }

  /// A parameter of a tile (a city, units): a `key` the widget declares under
  /// `[[widgets.params]]` in plugin.toml, and its value.
  record widget-param {
    key: string,
    value: string,
  }

  /// A tile `search` shows for its query. `id` names a `[[widgets]]` entry of plugin.toml.
  /// `params`: only keys that widget declares, each at most once, all values together at
  /// most 4 KiB; they come back in the tile's events and render contexts, and "Add to Home"
  /// puts the tile on the user's Home with them. `size` is one of the widget's `sizes`;
  /// none means the first. `actions` work as an item's (at most 16; the first is Enter's).
  /// An answer that breaks any of these rules is rejected as a whole.
  record widget-answer {
    id: string,
    params: list<widget-param>,
    size: option<widget-size>,
    actions: list<action>,
  }

  /// At most 50 items and 16 widget answers; a longer answer is rejected as a whole.
  record search-answer {
    items: list<item>,
    widgets: list<widget-answer>,
  }

  enum color-token {
    text,
    muted,
    panel,
    border,
    selected,
    error,
    success,
  }

  /// A theme colour, or `0xRRGGBBAA`.
  variant color {
    token(color-token),
    rgba(u32),
  }

  record theme-colors {
    text: u32,
    muted: u32,
    panel: u32,
    border: u32,
    selected: u32,
    error: u32,
    success: u32,
  }

  record text-sizes {
    title: f32,
    value: f32,
    body: f32,
    caption: f32,
  }

  record widget-theme {
    dark: bool,
    colors: theme-colors,
    ui-font: string,
    mono-font: string,
    sizes: text-sizes,
  }

  record point {
    x: f32,
    y: f32,
  }

  /// The pointer over the tile, in the tile's logical pixels; `node-id` is the canvas under it.
  record pointer-at {
    node-id: option<string>,
    at: point,
  }

  /// What `render-widget` is told. `millis-shown` counts from when the tile came on screen.
  /// `query` is set for a search tile only. `params` are the tile's: those of its search
  /// answer, or of its entry on the user's Home (only the keys the widget declares).
  record widget-context {
    width: f32,
    height: f32,
    scale: f32,
    theme: widget-theme,
    millis-shown: u64,
    focused: option<string>,
    pointer: option<pointer-at>,
    query: option<string>,
    params: list<widget-param>,
  }

  record edges {
    top: f32,
    right: f32,
    bottom: f32,
    left: f32,
  }

  enum align {
    start,
    center,
    end,
    stretch,
  }

  enum justify {
    start,
    center,
    end,
    space-between,
  }

  record layout {
    padding: edges,
    gap: f32,
    align: align,
    justify: justify,
  }

  enum text-style {
    title,
    value,
    body,
    caption,
  }

  enum text-align {
    start,
    center,
    end,
  }

  /// `text` is at most 4 KiB and may hold line breaks (no other control characters);
  /// `max-lines` 0 means 1.
  record text-node {
    text: string,
    style: text-style,
    color: color,
    align: text-align,
    max-lines: u8,
    mono: bool,
  }

  record icon-node {
    icon: icon,
    size: f32,
  }

  enum image-fit {
    contain,
    cover,
    fill,
  }

  record image-node {
    image: string,
    fit: image-fit,
    radius: f32,
  }

  /// Focusable; pressing it sends `widget-event.action` with `action-id`.
  record button-node {
    label: option<string>,
    icon: option<icon>,
    action-id: string,
  }

  /// Commands draw in order, in the node's logical pixels, clipped to the node. The caps
  /// are not a speed promise: a canvas that paints slowly is stopped, like a widget that
  /// draws late; aim for at most about 1,000 primitives per tile.
  record canvas-node {
    focusable: bool,
    commands: list<draw>,
  }

  variant node-kind {
    row(layout),
    column(layout),
    overlay(layout),
    text(text-node),
    icon(icon-node),
    image(image-node),
    progress(f32),
    spacer,
    button(button-node),
    canvas(canvas-node),
  }

  /// Node 0 is the root (`parent: none`); every other node names an earlier row, column
  /// or overlay. Nodes nest at most 32 deep: a node has at most 32 ancestors.
  /// `width`/`height` fix the size in logical pixels; `grow` shares free space. An overlay
  /// takes its size from its first child; later children are stacked on it at the same
  /// origin, inset by the overlay's padding.
  record node {
    id: string,
    parent: option<u32>,
    grow: f32,
    width: option<f32>,
    height: option<f32>,
    kind: node-kind,
  }

  record color-stop {
    color: color,
    at: f32,
  }

  /// Two stops only; `angle` in degrees, 0 is up, clockwise (CSS). The gradient spans the
  /// axis-aligned box of the shape as drawn, its direction turned with the shape.
  record linear-gradient {
    angle: f32,
    start: color-stop,
    end: color-stop,
  }

  variant paint {
    solid(color),
    linear(linear-gradient),
  }

  /// Filled when `stroke` is none (non-zero winding), else stroked that wide, centred on the
  /// outline; a transform scales the width by the mean of its two scales. `opacity`
  /// multiplies the paint.
  record shape-style {
    paint: paint,
    stroke: option<f32>,
    opacity: f32,
  }

  record rect-cmd {
    x: f32,
    y: f32,
    width: f32,
    height: f32,
    radius: f32,
    style: shape-style,
  }

  record ellipse-cmd {
    cx: f32,
    cy: f32,
    rx: f32,
    ry: f32,
    style: shape-style,
  }

  record quad-to {
    ctrl: point,
    to: point,
  }

  record cubic-to {
    ctrl-a: point,
    ctrl-b: point,
    to: point,
  }

  variant path-segment {
    move-to(point),
    line-to(point),
    quad-to(quad-to),
    cubic-to(cubic-to),
    close,
  }

  record path-cmd {
    segments: list<path-segment>,
    style: shape-style,
  }

  /// One line, at most 1 KiB; `x`/`y` is the top-left of its line box, 1.25 × `size` high.
  /// `size` is 1 to 200, and at most 200 after a transform's scale too. `font` none: the
  /// theme's UI font (`mono`: its monospace font). Emoji glyphs ignore `opacity`.
  record text-cmd {
    x: f32,
    y: f32,
    text: string,
    size: f32,
    color: color,
    font: option<string>,
    mono: bool,
    opacity: f32,
  }

  record image-cmd {
    image: string,
    x: f32,
    y: f32,
    width: f32,
    height: f32,
    opacity: f32,
  }

  record clip-rect {
    x: f32,
    y: f32,
    width: f32,
    height: f32,
  }

  /// Applied as scale, then rotate (degrees, clockwise), then translate. Shapes and paths take
  /// all of it; text, images and SVG take only the translation and the scale. A clip under a
  /// rotation clips the axis-aligned box around the rotated rectangle.
  record transform {
    translate: point,
    scale: point,
    rotate: f32,
  }

  variant draw {
    rect(rect-cmd),
    ellipse(ellipse-cmd),
    path(path-cmd),
    text(text-cmd),
    image(image-cmd),
    svg(image-cmd),
    push-clip(clip-rect),
    pop-clip,
    push-transform(transform),
    pop-transform,
  }

  enum image-format {
    png,
    jpeg,
    svg,
  }

  /// Bytes a tile names by `id`: PNG or JPEG of at most 1024x1024 pixels, or an SVG document
  /// with no external references, scripts or external fonts (only `data:` PNG/JPEG images
  /// inside). Caps: 2 MiB per image, 512 KiB per SVG, 64 images and 8 MiB per plugin;
  /// replacing an id frees the old bytes. An SVG has at most 4096 elements (counted again
  /// once `<use>` copies are made), 32 filter primitives, and 32 primitives applied in all
  /// (a filter on many elements counts once per element); at most 16 raster images drawn
  /// (each `<use>` again), 4 Mpx decoded in all; `feTurbulence` at most 10 octaves,
  /// `feMorphology` a radius of at most 32 device pixels, `feConvolveMatrix` an order of at
  /// most 9. A filter region (in device pixels) is at most twice the raster's size. Text
  /// uses only the fonts it names: a character none of them has is not drawn. A document
  /// that breaks these rules, or takes more than 16 ms to rasterize, is shown as nothing.
  record widget-image {
    id: string,
    format: image-format,
    bytes: list<u8>,
  }

  /// At most 256 nodes, 10 000 canvas commands and path segments, 1 MiB in all (images
  /// included); clips and transforms balance per canvas and nest at most 32 deep. `actions`
  /// are a Home tile's (a search tile's come from its answer). `animate`: render
  /// again on the next frame. A frame that breaks a cap is dropped and logged.
  record widget-frame {
    nodes: list<node>,
    animate: bool,
    actions: list<action>,
    images: list<widget-image>,
  }

  enum pointer-kind {
    down,
    up,
    move,
    enter,
    leave,
  }

  record widget-pointer {
    node-id: string,
    kind: pointer-kind,
    at: point,
  }

  flags modifiers {
    ctrl,
    alt,
    shift,
    super,
  }

  record widget-key {
    node-id: string,
    key: string,
    modifiers: modifiers,
  }

  record widget-action {
    node-id: option<string>,
    action-id: string,
  }

  /// `action`, `key` and a completed click (an `up` on the node that got the last `down`) are user
  /// actions; a `down` alone, a move, `enter`, `leave`, `shown` and `hidden` are not.
  variant widget-event {
    shown,
    hidden,
    action(widget-action),
    pointer(widget-pointer),
    key(widget-key),
  }

  /// An event of a tile, with the tile's parameters. Two tiles of the same widget with the
  /// same parameters are the same to the plugin.
  record widget-target {
    widget-id: string,
    params: list<widget-param>,
    event: widget-event,
  }

  /// One of the plugin's own tiles on the user's Home: the widget and its parameters (only
  /// the keys the widget declares).
  record home-tile {
    widget-id: string,
    params: list<widget-param>,
  }

  /// Something a plugin asks the launcher to do. Effects are applied in
  /// order.
  ///
  /// In answer to a view event (`on-event` with `event.view`):
  /// - `push-view` shows a new view on top;
  /// - `update-view` updates the view the event came from, following the
  ///   revision rules of `list-view`, `scratchpad-view` and `form-view`; an
  ///   update with another id or of another kind is dropped;
  /// - `pop-view` pops the view the event came from, never another one;
  /// - `close-window` hides the launcher.
  ///
  /// In answer to `run`, which no view sent, and to a widget event that is a
  /// user action: `push-view` and `close-window` as above; `update-view`
  /// updates the topmost view with that id that belongs to the plugin;
  /// `pop-view` pops the top view only if it belongs to the plugin.
  ///
  /// Unsolicited, that is returned from `init` or from `on-event` for a
  /// timer, an HTTP answer, a preference change, the Home tiles (`home`) or a
  /// widget event that is not a user action:
  /// - `update-view` updates the topmost view on the stack with that id that
  ///   belongs to the plugin, and is dropped if there is none;
  /// - `push-view` is honoured only while the launcher window is open and its
  ///   top view belongs to the plugin; a push of a view id the plugin has on
  ///   the stack (which returns to that view, see "Always") is honoured only
  ///   when every view above that one belongs to the plugin too;
  /// - at most 30 unsolicited `push-view` and `update-view` per s, counted
  ///   together;
  /// - at most 30 unsolicited `set-widget-image` per s;
  /// - `pop-view` and `close-window` are ignored;
  /// - in answer to a widget event that is not a user action, `push-view`,
  ///   `pop-view` and `close-window` are dropped, with the denial toast;
  /// - `copy-text` is dropped: it needs a user action;
  /// - at most 1 `replace-items` per 5 s (`init`'s counts).
  ///
  /// Always:
  /// - a `push-view` whose `id` names a view this plugin already has on the
  ///   stack does not stack a copy: it returns to that view. The views above
  ///   it are popped and get their `closed` (a scratchpad's last edit arrives
  ///   as `text-changed` first), and the view is replaced by the pushed one,
  ///   as a new push: its text, query, selection and revision start from the
  ///   pushed view, and answers to events of the old one are dropped. The
  ///   replaced view gets no `closed`; a scratchpad's waiting edit is sent as
  ///   `text-changed` before it is replaced;
  /// - `toast` is shown only while the launcher window is open (see `toast`);
  /// - `copy-text` copies to the clipboard, also while the launcher window is
  ///   hidden; it needs the `clipboard` permission and a user action (only in
  ///   answer to `run`, `action-chosen`, `form-submitted` or a widget user
  ///   action; dropped in answer to `query-changed`, `text-changed`, `closed`
  ///   and the widget events that are not user actions);
  /// - `replace-items` replaces the items this plugin supplied before (from
  ///   earlier `replace-items`); the items of the commands in `plugin.toml`
  ///   belong to the host and are not affected. It needs the `index`
  ///   permission, and at most 1000 items.
  /// - One answer pushes at most one view: a second `push-view` in the same
  ///   list is dropped (with the denial toast), the other effects are kept.
  /// - The launcher keeps at most 32 pushed views on its stack; a push beyond
  ///   is dropped.
  ///
  /// Widgets, in answer to any call that returns effects (`init`, `run` and
  /// `on-event`):
  /// - `redraw-widget` asks for a new `render-widget` of every tile of that
  ///   widget, whatever its parameters; the widget must be declared, else the
  ///   whole answer is rejected; beyond 60 per s it is merged into the next
  ///   render;
  /// - `set-widget-image` makes `widget-image` available to the plugin's
  ///   frames by its id (see `widget-image` for the caps).
  ///
  /// Preferences, in answer to any call that returns effects:
  /// - `preference-default` says what an unset preference stands for: `key`
  ///   is a preference the plugin declares in `plugin.toml`, `value` what the
  ///   plugin uses while the user has set none, as `preferences.get` would
  ///   answer it (for a `bool`, `true` or `false`; for a `number`, a number
  ///   within its bounds; for a `choice`, one of its option values; at most
  ///   1 KiB). The settings window shows it as the preference's value until
  ///   the user picks one, in place of the manifest's `default`; nothing is
  ///   written to `config.toml`. Of several for one key in one answer, the
  ///   last counts; the launcher keeps the last value per key while this
  ///   instance runs. An empty `value` takes the key's value back: the window
  ///   shows the manifest's `default` again, or that the value is not known.
  ///   An undeclared key or a value that does not fit is dropped (logged, no
  ///   toast).
  variant effect {
    push-view(view),
    update-view(view),
    pop-view,
    close-window,
    toast(toast),
    copy-text(string),
    replace-items(list<item>),
    redraw-widget(string),
    set-widget-image(widget-image),
    preference-default(key-value),
  }

  type request-id = u64;
  type timer-id = u64;

  record http-request {
    method: string,
    url: string,
    headers: list<key-value>,
    body: option<list<u8>>,
  }

  record http-response {
    status: u16,
    headers: list<key-value>,
    body: list<u8>,
  }

  /// A view event and the id of the view it came from.
  record view-target {
    view-id: string,
    event: view-event,
  }

  /// Delivered to `on-event`.
  variant event {
    view(view-target),
    timer-fired(timer-id),
    http-done(http-done),
    preferences-changed,
    widget(widget-target),
    /// The answer to `location.request`: where the computer is, or why that is not known.
    location(result<coordinates, string>),
    /// The plugin's own tiles on the user's Home, each widget and parameters once, in Home
    /// order; nothing of other plugins' tiles. Sent after `init` and whenever that list
    /// changes (a tile added or removed, `config.toml` edited, the plugin enabled again);
    /// when several changes wait, only the latest list is delivered. A background event,
    /// never a user action: keep the tiles' data fresh with it while the launcher is
    /// hidden (hidden tiles get no `render-widget`).
    home(list<home-tile>),
  }

  /// A place in degrees: `latitude` −90 to 90, `longitude` −180 to 180;
  /// `accuracy-km`, how far the real place may be from it.
  record coordinates {
    latitude: f64,
    longitude: f64,
    accuracy-km: u32,
  }

  record http-done {
    request: request-id,
    response: result<http-response, string>,
  }
}

/// The plugin's own key-value store; needs the `storage` permission.
/// Quota: 1 MiB in total, counted as the bytes of every key and value plus
/// 32 bytes per entry; keys at most 256 bytes, values at most 256 KiB. A
/// `set` refused because the quota would be exceeded fails with an error
/// that starts with `quota: `; no other error starts so.
/// Every function fails without the permission. At most 20 writes (`set` and
/// `remove` together, failed ones included) per second; the writes beyond
/// fail. Keep data in memory and write it when it changes, not on a timer.
interface storage {
  get: func(key: string) -> result<option<string>, string>;
  /// Also fails when the quota would be exceeded or the write rate is.
  set: func(key: string, value: string) -> result<_, string>;
  /// Also fails when the write rate is exceeded.
  remove: func(key: string) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// The plugin's preferences, declared in `plugin.toml` and set by the user.
/// `get` answers the user's value, else the manifest default, else `none`;
/// an undeclared key is `none`. A `bool` preference arrives as the text
/// `"true"` or `"false"`. A change arrives as `event.preferences-changed`.
interface preferences {
  get: func(key: string) -> option<string>;
}

/// Opens links and files with the user's default application; needs the
/// `open` permission and a user action: both functions fail unless called
/// during `run`, `on-event` with an `action-chosen` or `form-submitted`
/// view event, or `on-event` with a widget event that is a user action (not
/// during `init`, `search`, `render-widget`, a `query-changed`,
/// `text-changed` or `closed` view event, a widget event that is not a user
/// action, or a timer, HTTP or preference event). One call opens at most
/// once: after one URL or path has been opened, both functions fail until the
/// call returns.
interface open {
  /// Only `http`, `https` and `mailto` URLs, without spaces or control
  /// characters and without a user name or password. A `mailto` URL keeps only
  /// its `to`, `cc`, `bcc`, `subject` and `body` fields; others (such as
  /// `attach`) are removed.
  open-url: func(url: string) -> result<_, string>;
  /// Only absolute paths to existing files and folders that are not
  /// executable. Refused on every OS: a file with an exec bit, and these
  /// extensions in any case: exe bat cmd ps1 lnk msi com scr vbs js pif cpl hta
  /// jse vbe wsf wsh msc msp reg url app command tool terminal workflow action
  /// prefpane pkg mpkg scpt dmg webloc inetloc fileloc desktop appimage
  /// flatpakref deb rpm sh py pl rb jar. Symbolic links are followed, and both
  /// the link's name and the target are checked. Any refused path fails with
  /// the same error, `this path may not be opened`.
  open-path: func(path: string) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Web requests to the domains allowed in `plugin.toml`. Only `http` and
/// `https`; at most 4 requests at a time (a request counts until its
/// `http-done` is delivered). The launcher runs the requests of all plugins
/// on a few shared workers; waiting requests of different plugins take turns.
/// The response body is at most 4 MiB, a larger
/// one is an error; a request times out 30 s after `fetch`, waiting for the
/// launcher, redirects and the body included; at most 5 redirects are
/// followed, and every hop must match the allowed domains. A status code other
/// than 2xx is an answer, not an error. When the plugin stops or is unloaded,
/// its requests that have not started are not sent and the answers of running
/// ones are discarded.
///
/// The domain is matched on the parsed URL. URLs with a user name or password
/// are refused; an IPv4 address matches only an allowed entry written as that
/// address (a wildcard never does); IPv6 addresses are never allowed. A host
/// allowed by a name may not resolve to a local address (loopback, private,
/// unique-local, link-local, carrier-grade NAT, unspecified or multicast; an
/// IPv6 address that embeds an IPv4 address, IPv4-mapped, IPv4-compatible,
/// NAT64 `64:ff9b::/96` or 6to4 `2002::/16`, counts as that address),
/// checked on every connection, every hop included: only `localhost` and an
/// entry written as an IP address reach local addresses. Proxies from the
/// environment are not used. The
/// method is one of GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE and OPTIONS (GET and
/// HEAD carry no body). The launcher sets `host`, `content-length`,
/// `transfer-encoding`, `connection`, `keep-alive`, `upgrade`, `te`,
/// `trailer`, `expect`, `proxy-authorization` and `proxy-connection`; a
/// request that sets one of them is refused. A redirect to another origin
/// drops `authorization` and `cookie`; other headers the plugin set, including
/// its own credential headers (such as `x-api-key`), go to every hop, so every
/// host on the way must be one the plugin trusts with them (all hops are on
/// the allowed list).
interface http {
  use types.{http-request, request-id};
  /// Starts a request and returns at once; the answer arrives as `event.http-done`.
  /// Fails when the domain is not allowed, the request is not valid or the
  /// concurrent-request limit is reached.
  fetch: func(request: http-request) -> result<request-id, string>;
}

/// Timers; need the `timers` permission. At most 16 active timers per
/// plugin; a repeating timer's interval is at least 250 ms, a one-shot
/// delay at least 10 ms. A one-shot timer is active until its `timer-fired`
/// is delivered or it is cancelled. While a repeating timer's `timer-fired`
/// waits for a busy plugin, its next firings are skipped, not queued. Timer
/// ids are not reused; cancelling an unknown id does nothing.
///
/// Timers end with the plugin instance (an unload, a reload, a stop): re-arm
/// them in `init`. The host may delay, coalesce or suspend timers, especially
/// while the launcher is hidden, and suspends them when the plugin is over its
/// background budget (see the package docs).
interface timers {
  use types.{timer-id};
  /// Fires `event.timer-fired` after `millis` at the earliest; a repeating
  /// timer fires until cancelled or until its plugin instance ends.
  /// Fails when the quota is exceeded or the interval is too short.
  start: func(millis: u64, repeat: bool) -> result<timer-id, string>;
  cancel: func(timer: timer-id);
}

/// The user's locale; needs no permission.
interface locale {
  /// The IANA name of the time zone the computer is set to (`Europe/Moscow`), read when
  /// called, so it follows a change; `UTC` when the launcher cannot tell. At most 64 bytes of
  /// `A-Z a-z 0-9 / _ + -`.
  local-time-zone: func() -> string;

  /// The languages the computer is set to, best first: ISO 639 codes of 2–3 lowercase
  /// ASCII letters (`ru`, `en`, `kk`), without repeats, at most 16; empty when the launcher
  /// cannot tell. Read when called, so it follows a change. The launcher takes, in turn,
  /// the operating system's preferred languages (on Linux `LANGUAGE`, `LC_ALL`,
  /// `LC_MESSAGES` and `LANG`; on macOS and Windows the user's preferred languages) and, on
  /// Linux, the languages of the KDE keyboard layouts. A region or script is dropped:
  /// `en_US.UTF-8` is `en`, `zh-Hans-CN` is `zh`.
  languages: func() -> list<string>;
}

/// The computer's approximate location; needs the `location` permission. The
/// launcher takes it from, in turn: the city the user set, the operating
/// system's location service, the Wi-Fi networks around (only when the user
/// turned that on), the time zone.
interface location {
  /// Asks where the computer is. The answer arrives later as `event.location`:
  /// the coordinates rounded to 0.1°, with `accuracy-km` never below 11 (a
  /// time zone's place is 500), or why there are none. A request while one of
  /// the plugin's waits is answered by the same event. The launcher finds the
  /// location at most once at a time for all plugins and keeps its answer for
  /// 10 minutes (a coarser one than 50 km, or a failure, for 1 minute), so
  /// asking often never reaches a source more often. When the user changes the
  /// launcher's location settings, a plugin that asked before gets the new
  /// answer as an event, unasked. The event is not a user action. Fails at
  /// once without the permission.
  request: func() -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Logging into the launcher's log. Messages are truncated to 4 KiB, and at
/// most 100 lines per s are kept.
interface log {
  enum level { debug, info, warn, error }
  log: func(level: level, message: string);
}

world plugin {
  use types.{item, effect, event, query-reading, search-answer, widget-context, widget-frame};

  import storage;
  import preferences;
  import open;
  import http;
  import timers;
  import log;
  import locale;
  import location;

  /// Called once after the user approved the plugin and it was loaded, and
  /// again after every reload. The host indexes the commands of
  /// `plugin.toml` before it calls `init`.
  export init: func() -> result<list<effect>, string>;
  /// Dynamic search, for plugins with `search = true` under `[permissions]`
  /// in `plugin.toml` (the review prompt says "See what you type in the
  /// launcher search"); without the permission it is never called: items
  /// and widget answers (tiles) computed for `query`. There is no rank:
  /// results the user has not chosen before keep the order returned, and the
  /// user's usage can move a result up. A result whose id is also an indexed
  /// item is not shown. At most 50 items and 16 widget answers: a longer
  /// answer is rejected as a whole (see `search-answer`). Called on every
  /// keystroke; answers that arrive after the next keystroke are dropped.
  /// `readings`: at most 8, never equal to `query` or to each other.
  export search: func(query: string, readings: list<query-reading>) -> result<search-answer, string>;
  /// Runs `action-id` on `item`, the whole item the user chose, indexed or
  /// returned by `search`, so a dynamic result needs no lookup. A command
  /// declared in `plugin.toml` arrives as the item `<plugin-id>/<command-id>`
  /// with the action id `run`.
  export run: func(item: item, action-id: string) -> result<list<effect>, string>;
  /// Delivers a view event, a timer, an HTTP answer, a preference change, a
  /// widget event, a location or the plugin's Home tiles.
  export on-event: func(event: event) -> result<list<effect>, string>;
  /// Draws a tile of the widget `id`, declared under `[[widgets]]` in
  /// `plugin.toml`. Called by the launcher only, at most 60 times per s per
  /// tile, while the tile is on screen or about to be shown. It should answer
  /// within 16 ms (a later frame is dropped) and must within 100 ms, see the
  /// package docs.
  export render-widget: func(id: string, context: widget-context) -> result<widget-frame, string>;
}
